# CSS in Markup

The class names you type into HTML and the custom properties you set on a
`style` attribute. Everything else about CSS is `css-standards.md`, scoped to
`**/*.css`, because new CSS goes in a `.css` file loaded with `stylesheet_tag`,
not in the Liquid (`{% stylesheet %}` is banned in new sections, `sections.md`).

## Namespace Your CSS Variables

Namespace variables unless they are meant to bleed into other components.

```css
/* Do this */
.component {
  --component-padding: ...;
  --component-aspect-ratio: ...;
}

/* Not this */
.component {
  --padding: ...;
  --aspect-ratio: ...;
}
```

## Scoping CSS to Instances of Sections and Blocks

Pass section and block settings in as custom properties on a `style`
attribute; the CSS stays in the asset stylesheet and no per-instance selector
is generated.

✅ Do this:

```html
<section
  style="
    --background-color: {{ settings.background_color }};
    --padding: {{ settings.padding }}px;
  "
>
  ...
</section>

<button style="--button-color: {{ settings.button_color }};">...</button>
```

❌ Don't do this:

```html
{% style %} .selector--{{ block.id }} { --button-color: {{ settings.button_color }}; } {% endstyle %}

<button class="selector--{{ block.id }}">...</button>
```

## BEM Naming Convention

- **Block**: the component (`.product-card`)
- **Element**: block + `__` + element (`.product-card__title`)
- **Modifier**: block or element + `--` + modifier (`.product-card--featured`)
- Dashes separate words at every level (`.block__multi-word-element--multi-word-modifier`)

Global utility classes are the one exception to BEM.

### Naming a "Block" (component)

The block wraps every element derived from it.

```html
<!-- Do this -->
<div class="my-component">
  <div class="my-component__wrapper"></div>
</div>

<!-- Not this: the element is the parent of its block -->
<div class="my-component__wrapper my-component--page-width">
  <div class="my-component"></div>
</div>
```

### Naming an "Element" (child)

One `__` per class name. Join further words with `-`, or start a new block
when the child could stand alone.

```html
<!-- Do this -->
<button class="my-component__button">
  <span class="my-component__button-label">My button</span>
</button>

<!-- Or this: a new scope -->
<button class="button-component">
  <span class="button-component__label">My button</span>
</button>

<!-- Not this -->
<button class="my-component__wrapper__button">
  <span class="my-component__wrapper__button__label">My button</span>
</button>
```

### Naming a "Modifier" (variant)

A modifier uses `--` and sits beside its base class, never alone and never as
a second exclusive block name.

```html
<!-- Do this -->
<button class="button button--secondary"></button>

<!-- Not this -->
<button class="button button-secondary"></button>
<button class="button--secondary"></button>
```

Put the modifier on the highest element that makes sense, so children can
respond to it later:

```html
<div class="my-component my-component--size-large my-component--page-width">
  <div class="my-component__wrapper"></div>
</div>
```

### Utility Classes

A global override for a single decision (alignment, show/hide), with no
hierarchy and no assumption about the block it sits on. Multi-word names use
`-`, with any viewport suffix at the **end**: `hidden-mobile`.

```css
.align-left {
  text-align: left;
}
```

```html
<div class="my-component align-left">
  <p class="my-component__text"></p>
</div>
```
